      ":question/problem": "If ${4}^{k-1}={64}^{2k}$ then $k=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${4}^{3},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{k-1} & ={64}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={({4}^{3})}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={4}^{6k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-1 & =6k \\\\ -1 & =5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
