      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-3103",
      ":question/problem": "If ${5}^{2a-5}=\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}}$ then $a=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $25$ as ${5}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{({5}^{2})}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{2(a+1)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & ={5}^{-2(a+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5 & =-2(a+1) \\\\ 2a-5 & =-2a-2 \\\\ 4a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
