      ":question/problem": "Solve for $x$ where ${4}^{x+4}={16}^{x}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $16$ as ${4}^{2},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+4} & ={16}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={({4}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {4}^{x+4} & ={4}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"
